cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it."
title: "Inference without trained weights"
abstract: "A vertex's coordinate and a relation's weight are derived from a store of statements at the moment a question is asked, in one shared basis, with no fitted constant. A vertex's coordinate is what it is connected to, recomputed from the edges and stored nowhere; a relation's weight is the cross-covariance of the statements that use it; and attention is an alignment standardized by the exact permutation null, with no temperature and no forced normalization. A fact enters by one write and the next read has it, with nothing retrained. The protocol makes the claim falsifiable: the statement asked about is removed from both the operator and the coordinate before anything is computed, and every answer reports whether the store, as read, still contains it. On a hand-written store of 84 statements the bare operator names 36.8% of 68 held-out facts from a pool averaging 64.7 candidates, 23.8 times a draw over the same candidates. It survives a lookup arm and a degree-preserving permutation null on two of three WordNet neighbourhoods and does not clear the null on the third. A TransE model retrained for every held-out question and tuned over dimension and margin does better at its best, at 53.4%, and on text the read does not beat counting. The algebra the engine rests on is machine-checked in Lean 4 against Mathlib, among it that the operator never has to be formed, that a statement arriving adds one rank-one term, and that the permutation null has mean zero and a closed-form second moment. Relation matrices are not new; RESCAL, DistMult, R-GCN and linearized attention all carry one, obtained by gradient descent with the relation index supplied. Here the matrix is measured and the index does not exist."
